Data files for the games.

games/bsd/COPYING states the terms for the game SOURCE.  The data those
games read lives here, one directory away, and most of it is Berkeley's on the
same terms.  A reader who found COPYING next to the source would not learn
that, so this file says it.


BERKELEY / NetBSD, on the terms in ../bsd/COPYING
-------------------------------------------------

    quiz.db/            35 files, the NetBSD/Debian bsdgames quiz database.
                        See the separate notice below -- this is the one
                        part of the directory that carries its own grant.
    atc/                16 air-traffic-control scenarios, NetBSD's verbatim.
                        atc additionally carries Ed James 1987 terms; see
                        ../bsd/COPYING, which already names them.
    cribbage.instr      BSD games/cribbage/instr
    fish.instr          BSD games/fish/fish.instr
    wump.info           BSD games/wump/wump.info
    monop.inp           BSD games/monop/cards.inp

    adventure.msg       NOT a copy -- generated here from ../bsd/adventure/
                        glorkz by dumper.c.host, and checked in.  Its parent
                        is BSD adventure, which per ../bsd/COPYING is the
                        FOUR-clause BSD form (with the advertising clause)
                        plus Copyright (c) 1997 Christos Zoulas.  That is the
                        strictest term in the games tree, and being a binary
                        blob this file gives no sign of it.  Noted for that
                        reason.


quiz.db/Makefrag carries its own grant
--------------------------------------

Reproduced from quiz.db/Makefrag, lines 3-27, which is 3-clause BSD:

    # Copyright (c) 1997, 1998, 1999 Joseph Samuel Myers.
    # All rights reserved.
    #
    # Redistribution and use in source and binary forms, with or without
    # modification, are permitted provided that the following conditions
    # are met:
    # 1. Redistributions of source code must retain the above copyright
    #    notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer.
    # 2. Redistributions in binary form must reproduce the above copyright
    #    notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer in the
    #    documentation and/or other materials provided with the distribution.
    # 3. The name of the author may not be used to endorse or promote products
    #    derived from this software without specific prior written permission.

    (the standard BSD disclaimer follows it in that file)

quiz.db/index.in still carries the unsubstituted autoconf token `@quiz_dir@'
on every line, which is how the Debian bsdgames lineage was established.


MARK WILLIAMS COHERENT, on the terms in the root LICENSE
--------------------------------------------------------

    fortunes            363 entries separated by blank lines, which is the
                        format COHERENT's own fortune(1) reads.  MWC's,
                        byte-identical to the 4.2 holding, and the Lexicon's
                        fortune page describes it.  It replaced NetBSD's
                        583,924-byte fortunes.big and the strfile index built
                        from it, which is why no BSD fortune database is
                        listed above any more.
    rubik.m4
    kl                  (zero bytes upstream too)


WRITTEN FOR THIS PORT
---------------------

    src/                2 K&R shims for the 3.2 libc -- err.c and fgetln.c.
                        BSD API names, implementations written here; each
                        says so in its own header.  The other nine shims are
                        gone: libc has all of them, and a source on the link
                        line was shadowing the library's.
    cursrc/             curs_set.c, waddch.c -- overrides for a big-endian
                        Z8001 bug in the in-tree libcurses.
    hangman.words       19357 words derived from COHERENT's own spell
                        dictionary, not from BSD (the tree has no
                        /usr/dict/words); see ../bsd/hangman/hangman.h.
